{"qid":"Q208169","names":["Republic of Ragusa"],"title":"Republic of Ragusa","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Republic of Ragusa was an aristocratic maritime city-state centred on the walled city of Ragusa (modern Dubrovnik), which secured practical independence in 1358 by the Treaty of Zadar, transferring from Venetian to Hungarian suzerainty while governing itself through a Great Council of noble families who elected a Rector on monthly terms. Its merchant fleet and diplomatic finesse made it one of the wealthiest Adriatic states: Ragusa pioneered abolition of the slave trade in 1416, maintained embassies in Istanbul and Madrid simultaneously, and from 1458 paid an annual Ottoman tribute of 12,500 gold ducats in exchange for free access to Ottoman markets. A catastrophic earthquake on 6 April 1667 killed the Rector and some 5,000 citizens and permanently diminished the republic's commercial power. Marshal Marmont occupied the city in 1806 and formally abolished the republic in January 1808, incorporating its territory into Napoleon's Illyrian Provinces.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The republic's territory — the city, its coastal strip (Astarea), the Pelješac peninsula, and offshore islands — is precisely documented in Ragusan chancery records and early modern cartography.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Republic of Ragusa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Ragusa"},{"label":"Britannica: Dubrovnik","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Dubrovnik"},{"label":"Britannica: Republic of Ragusa","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Republic-of-Ragusa"}],"britannica":null,"